upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about settlement types, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Nucleated settlements are most typical of rural areas such as the highlands of Malaysia and the scrublands of central Australia, while dispersed settlements always include collective amenities like markets and schools","Dispersed settlements are one-family or few-family residences scattered in isolated places, separated by open country or geographical barriers","Nucleated settlements are groupings of several family residences with collective amenities such as shophouses, markets, schools and places of worship","Nucleated settlements may be called villages, towns, ports or cities depending on their size and function"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual classification — the text explicitly describes DISPERSED (not nucleated) settlements as most typical of rural highlands/scrublands, while nucleated settlements are the ones with collective amenities like markets and schools. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"According to Goh Cheng Leong, what is the key distinction between the 'site' and the 'situation' (or position) of a town, using Kuala Lumpur as the example?","o":["Site refers to the town's immediate topographical location (e.g., the confluence of the Sungai Kelang and Gombak), while situation refers to its locational relationship to the wider region (e.g., within the 'rubber and tin belt' of central Peninsular Malaysia)","Site and situation are interchangeable terms referring to the same topographical location","Site refers only to a town's regional economic role, while situation refers only to its physical landscape","Site refers to a town's population size, while situation refers to its administrative status"],"a":0,"e":"The text explicitly distinguishes site (the immediate topographical location, e.g., a river confluence) from situation/position (the town's relationship to the broader region, e.g., its place within a wider economic belt), cautioning readers not to confuse the two — not treating them as interchangeable or referring to population/administrative status."})

