upsc.append({"q":"Match List-I (Settlement Locational Factor, per Goh Cheng Leong) with List-II (Example Town) and select the correct answer using the code below.\nList-I:\nA. Site for generating hydro-electric power\nB. Mineral wealth\nC. Good site for defence\nD. Sheltered coastal indentation\nList-II:\n1. Johannesburg, the 'Golden City', founded on Witwatersrand gold discoveries\n2. Beijing, historically fortified with the Great Wall as its first line of defence\n3. Buffalo, which grew south of Niagara Falls\n4. Singapore, whose harbour is sheltered by Pulau Brani and Pulau Sentosa\nCodes:","o":["A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4","A-1, B-3, C-2, D-4","A-3, B-2, C-1, D-4","A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2"],"a":0,"e":"The correct match is A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4: Buffalo grew from Niagara Falls hydro-electric power; Johannesburg grew from gold discoveries; Beijing grew as a fortified defensive site; and Singapore's harbour is sheltered by Pulau Brani and Sentosa."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about river-borne settlement sites, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. A confluence town, such as Khartoum (at the meeting of the Blue and White Nile) or St. Louis (at the Mississippi-Missouri confluence), benefits from increased river volume and navigability.\n2. Geneva, where the River Rhône leaves Lake Geneva, illustrates a settlement taking advantage of both a river and a lake as lines of communication.\n3. Settlements never form at the point where a river enters or leaves a gorge, since the gorge itself is always too steep for any nearby settlement to develop.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect and reverses the actual pattern — the text explicitly gives examples like Bingen and Bonn (at either end of the Rhine Gorge) and Linz/Vienna (at either end of the Danube's gorge), where the valley widens at the gorge's entry/exit points, creating better settlement sites there, even though the gorge itself remains too steep. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

