upsc.append({"q":"Which one of the following statements about urbanization and conurbation, per Goh Cheng Leong, is NOT correct?","o":["Conurbations, where expanding towns merge into a continuous urban belt, are found only in developing countries and have never occurred in Europe or North America","The Midlands of England, where Birmingham, Wolverhampton and Walsall merge, form a well-known conurbation called the 'Black Country'","Since the beginning of the twentieth century, the ratio of urban to rural population has risen by more than 25% in most countries","In Australia, roughly three out of every four people live in towns"],"a":0,"e":"This statement is incorrect and reverses the actual examples — the text explicitly cites conurbations in DEVELOPED regions such as the Midlands of England ('Black Country'), the Great Lakes-St. Lawrence region of Canada, the U.S.A.'s Eastern seaboard (Megalopolis), and the Ruhr-Westphalia region of West Germany, not developing countries. The other three statements are correct."})

upsc.append({"q":"Consider the following statements about problems of urbanization, per Goh Cheng Leong:\n1. Rural depopulation, caused by migration to towns, can lead to abandoned farms and a scarcity of farm labour.\n2. Scarcity of urban land and exorbitant prices have necessitated multi-storey flats and skyscraper office blocks in congested cities.\n3. Traffic congestion problems in large cities occur only during a single peak period each day, typically the morning rush hour.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","o":["1 and 2 only","1 and 3 only","2 and 3 only","1, 2 and 3"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 3 is incorrect — the text explicitly describes congestion occurring during multiple periods: morning rush hours, lunch breaks, after office hours, and evenings when people go to restaurants and cinemas, not just a single morning peak. Statements 1 and 2 are correct."})

